.TH PROWAL 1 "2026-10-20" "prowal @PROJECT_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
prowal \- pop-up application launcher: a grid of icons around the mouse pointer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B prowal
.RB [ \-c
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-g
.IR name ]
.br
.B prowal \-\-edit
.RB [ \-c
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-g
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-group
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-item
.IR x,y ]
.br
.B prowal \-\-import\-apwal
.RI [ apwal-file ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-g
.IR name ]
.br
.B prowal
.BR \-h " | " \-V
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B prowal
shows a grid of icons centred on the mouse pointer and closes again once
something is launched.
Every icon keeps a fixed position relative to the pointer, so after a while
programs are started by muscle memory: press the key, flick the mouse, click.
.PP
Run without options it shows the grid called
.BR main .
Bind it to a key or a mouse button in the window manager.
.PP
.B prowal
is an X11 program; in a Wayland session it runs through XWayland.
It is a clean-room successor to
.BR apwal ,
whose layout it can import.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " \fIfile\fR"
Use
.I file
instead of
.IR ~/.config/prowal/prowal.json .
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-grid " \fIname\fR"
Show the grid called
.I name
instead of
.BR main .
With
.BR \-\-edit ,
open the editor on it; with
.BR \-\-import\-apwal ,
import into it.
.TP
.B \-\-edit
Open the editor.
If an editor for the same config file is already open, that one shows the
request (grid, group, item) and comes forward instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-group " path"
With
.BR \-\-edit :
open inside the group at cell
.IR x,y ;
nested groups are written
.IR x,y/x,y/... .
.TP
.BI \-\-item " x,y"
With
.BR \-\-edit :
select that cell.
.TP
.BR \-\-import\-apwal " [\fIapwal-file\fR]"
Import an apwal layout (default
.IR ~/.apwalrc.xml ),
replacing the items of the grid.
The apwal file is only read.
The previous config is backed up first.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the options and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.SH THE POPUP
.TP
.B Left click
Launch, then close (unless "keep open after launching" is set).
.TP
.B Middle click
Launch and keep the popup open.
.TP
.B Right click
Open the editor, with the clicked icon selected.
.TP
.BR "Arrow keys" ", " Home
Move the highlight; Home goes back to the middle.
While the keys are in use the popup does not time out.
.TP
.BR Enter ", " Space
Launch the highlighted icon or open the highlighted group.
.B Shift+Enter
keeps the popup open.
.TP
.BR Esc ", " Backspace
Go back up out of a group.
Esc at the top level closes the popup.
.TP
.B Click outside, or in a gap
Close.
.PP
Moving the pointer off the grid closes the popup after the timeout.
Programs that are not installed are shown greyed out.
.SS Groups
A group is an icon that opens its own grid of icons in place, centred on the
group's icon.
Groups can be nested.
If a group leaves its middle cell free, that cell becomes a Back button.
.SS Named grids
A config can hold several grids.
.B main
is the default;
.B prowal \-g
.I name
shows another, so different keys can open different launchers.
.SH THE EDITOR
.B prowal \-\-edit
(or a right click in the popup) opens the editor.
The dashed cell is where the pointer will be when the popup opens.
Drag icons to move them (onto another icon to swap), double-click to edit,
to add in an empty cell, or to open a group.
Programs can be added from the installed applications (Ctrl+I) or by dragging
.I .desktop
files, programs or images from a file manager.
Icons are chosen from every icon theme and icon folder (Choose\&...).
All changes can be undone (Ctrl+Z) and are written when saved (Ctrl+S).
.PP
Preferences (Ctrl+,) set the terminal used for launchers marked
"Run in a terminal", the close timeout, the look (floating icons or a panel,
icon size, spacing, labels, group icons, icon theme, colours, shadows) and the
animation.
Preferences are part of the config file.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/prowal/prowal.json
The layout and preferences (JSON).
Unknown keys are kept when the editor saves.
.TP
.I ~/.config/prowal/backups/
The previous config, copied there before each save; the last 10 are kept.
.TP
.I ~/.config/prowal/icon-picker.ini
The icon picker's folders and recently used icons.
.TP
.I ~/.apwalrc.xml
apwal's layout, imported on first run if it exists.
It is never written.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TERMINAL
On first run, the terminal to use if it names an installed program.
Otherwise Konsole, then the first installed of a list of common terminals.
.TP
.B PROWAL_TRACE_STARTUP
If set, print start-up timings to standard error.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where
.I prowal/
lives (default
.IR ~/.config ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 if the config can't be read or there is no display,
2 for bad command-line arguments.
.SH EXAMPLES
Xfce: Settings \(-> Keyboard \(-> Application Shortcuts, add
.B prowal
on a key.
A second launcher for games on another key:
.PP
.RS
.nf
prowal \-\-grid games
.fi
.RE
.PP
Bringing an apwal layout across into a grid of its own:
.PP
.RS
.nf
prowal \-\-import\-apwal ~/old/.apwalrc.xml \-\-grid old
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
If picom's fading is on it fades the popup in on top of Prowal's own
animation; turn it off for windows matching
.B class_g = 'prowal'
in
.IR picom.conf .
.SH LICENSE
